Orologi-Glow

Creative Catalyst: Musical instruments are fashioned from fallen meteorite shards encased in the clicking, calcified remains of Victorian clockwork.

Orologi-Glow is the sound of celestial debris oscillating through a gearbox. It feels like an airy, K-Indie bedroom pop production that has been disassembled and re-tuned by a mad watchmaker. The tenor saxophone is not blown in a traditional sense; the meteorite-lined bell creates a "gravity-drift" vibrato, where notes bend upward against the laws of physics, sounding simultaneously intimate and terrifyingly vast. It is music that suggests the stars are merely ticking mechanisms waiting to unwind.

Genre DNA
*   Tempo & Pulse: 62–74 BPM; a stuttering, non-linear rhythmic pulse that mimics a heartbeat failing to synchronize with a grandfather clock.
*   Harmonic Palette: Pentatonic scales disrupted by microtonal "gear-grind" dissonances and airy, open-fourth chords.
   Production Signatures: 1. Chronometric Quantization: intentionally misaligning the drum tracks by milliseconds; 2. Magnetic Tape Friction: running the master signal through iron filings recovered from meteorite craters; 3. Spatial Vacuum*: using custom convolution reverbs captured in empty planetarium domes.
*   Dynamics: A slow-boil structure, beginning as a minimalist, dry acoustic intimacy before expanding into an overwhelming, "pressurized" wall of airy, swirling metallic resonance.

Scene & Origin Lore
Born in the high-altitude abandoned observatories of Gangwon-do, Orologi-Glow was codified by a sect of itinerant musicians who call themselves "The Keepers of the Unwound." These artisans spend months meticulously polishing meteorite fragments into saxophone reeds and mechanical gear-housing. They wear layers of tattered, vintage silken tunics fused with brass cogs and clock-springs, treating every performance as a ritual of synchronization where the music must stop precisely when the moon crosses the local meridian. It is a fashion of frayed decadence, smelling of ozone and oxidation.

Central Instruments: Meteorite-encased Tenor Saxophone (The "Falling Lung"), Antique Brass Escapement-Synthesizers, Clock-spring bowed strings.

For Fans Of: Mid-Air Thief, Colin Stetson, Ichiko Aoba, Talk Talk.

Fictional Band Name: The Pendulum Parlor.

Band Description: A trio of reclusive virtuosos—a tenor saxophonist who performs in an isolation suit, a percussionist who plays a collection of synchronized ticking alarm clocks, and a vocalist who harmonizes with the humming resonance of the meteorite instruments. Their aesthetic is one of "Celestial Domesticity," looking like nomadic royalty lost in a Victorian sci-fi fever dream.

